Enable the JWT auth method in Vault

Tell Vault how to verify the JWT (public key / JWKS / OIDC-style issuer)

Create a Vault role that maps a JWT claim → Vault policies

Test it with curl

Mention how this works in an air-gapped setup

I’ll use CLI + curl so you can drop it in scripts.

1. Prereqs / what Vault needs

For Vault to trust a JWT, it needs one of these:

A JWKS URL (what you get from Keycloak, GitLab, Okta, etc.)

OR a public key (PEM) that matches the private key used to sign the JWT

OR an OIDC-style issuer (iss) + JWKS

In an air-gapped environment you usually don’t want Vault to reach out to the IdP over the internet, so you either:

host the JWKS internally, or

paste the public key directly into Vault.

I’ll show both.

2. Enable the JWT auth method
vault auth enable jwt


If it’s already enabled, you’ll get an error — that’s fine.

This creates: auth/jwt/

3. Configure the JWT auth method
Option A — you have an internal JWKS endpoint

(Example: https://keycloak.internal.lan/realms/dev/protocol/openid-connect/certs)

vault write auth/jwt/config \
  oidc_discovery_url="https://keycloak.internal.lan/realms/dev" \
  default_role="default"


Vault will discover the JWKS under that issuer.

Option B — air-gapped, use a static public key

If your app/team can hand you the public key (the one that matches the private key that signs tokens), do this:

Save it as jwt-pubkey.pem (PEM format, like -----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----).

Configure Vault:

vault write auth/jwt/config \
  jwt_validation_pubkeys=@jwt-pubkey.pem \
  default_role="default"


This tells Vault: “verify any incoming JWT with this key.”

4. Create a Vault policy (what the user can do)

Example policy dev-read-secrets.hcl:

path "secret/data/dev/*" {
  capabilities = ["read", "list"]
}


Load it:

vault policy write dev-read-secrets dev-read-secrets.hcl

5. Create a JWT role

This is the important part. The role tells Vault:

what aud to expect

what iss to expect (optional if you configured it above)

what claim to look at (email, sub, groups, etc.)

what Vault policies to give

Let’s say your JWTs look like this:

{
  "iss": "https://keycloak.internal.lan/realms/dev",
  "aud": "vault",
  "sub": "doris@example.com",
  "email": "doris@example.com",
  "groups": ["devops", "security"]
}


You can create a role that matches on aud=vault and gives the policy:

vault write auth/jwt/role/devops-role \
  role_type="jwt" \
  user_claim="email" \
  bound_audiences="vault" \
  bound_issuer="https://keycloak.internal.lan/realms/dev" \
  policies="dev-read-secrets" \
  ttl="1h"


What this means:

role_type=jwt → we’re using raw JWT, not full OIDC login flow

user_claim=email → Vault will treat the email from the token as the username

bound_audiences=vault → token must have "aud": "vault"

bound_issuer=... → token must come from this issuer

policies=... → what Vault lets this JWT do

ttl=1h → lease for the Vault token Vault will issue

If your token has groups and you want to match on that, you can add:

  claim_mappings=groups:groups


or use bound_claims, e.g.:

vault write auth/jwt/role/devops-role \
  role_type="jwt" \
  user_claim="email" \
  bound_audiences="vault" \
  bound_issuer="https://keycloak.internal.lan/realms/dev" \
  bound_claims='{"groups": ["devops"]}' \
  policies="dev-read-secrets" \
  ttl="1h"


Now only JWTs whose groups contains devops will work.

6. Test with a real JWT

Assume you already have a JWT in $JWT (maybe from Keycloak, GitLab CI, or your app):

JWT="$(cat mytoken.jwt)"


Call Vault’s login endpoint:

curl -s \
  --request POST \
  --data "{\"role\": \"devops-role\", \"jwt\": \"$JWT\"}" \
  http://127.0.0.1:8200/v1/auth/jwt/login


You should get back something like:

{
  "auth": {
    "client_token": "hvs.CAESIG2t....",
    "policies": ["default", "dev-read-secrets"],
    "lease_duration": 3600,
    "renewable": true
  }
}


That client_token is your Vault token. Use it like:

VAULT_TOKEN="hvs.CAESIG2t...."
curl -H "X-Vault-Token: $VAULT_TOKEN" \
  http://127.0.0.1:8200/v1/secret/data/dev/app1


If you get permission denied, check:

JWT aud matches bound_audiences

JWT iss matches bound_issuer

JWT is not expired

Policy actually allows the path

7. Using this in CI (GitLab / GitHub / K8s)

GitLab CI: GitLab can issue a JWT to the job (CI_JOB_JWT_V2). You just pass that token to Vault at job time and log in with the role that matches aud / iss.

Kubernetes: You can do the same with the Kubernetes auth method, but if you’re already issuing service-account JWTs and don’t want the k8s auth method, this JWT method works too — just point Vault to the cluster’s public key.

Air-gapped: don’t use oidc_discovery_url to the internet. Either host your IdP internally or paste the public key.

8. Security notes (the important parts)

Keep Vault over HTTPS (TLS) — JWTs are bearer tokens. If someone sniffs them, they can log in.

Keep Vault tokens short-lived (ttl=1h or less).

Use bound_audiences and bound_issuer. These two stop most “I found a JWT somewhere” abuses.

If your signer rotates keys, refresh jwt_validation_pubkeys (or point to JWKS if you can).

Full example (copy/paste)
# 1) enable
vault auth enable jwt

# 2) configure with static public key
vault write auth/jwt/config \
  jwt_validation_pubkeys=@jwt-pubkey.pem \
  default_role="devops-role"

# 3) policy
cat > dev-read-secrets.hcl <<'EOF'
path "secret/data/dev/*" {
  capabilities = ["read", "list"]
}
EOF

vault policy write dev-read-secrets dev-read-secrets.hcl

# 4) role
vault write auth/jwt/role/devops-role \
  role_type="jwt" \
  user_claim="email" \
  bound_audiences="vault" \
  bound_issuer="https://keycloak.internal.lan/realms/dev" \
  policies="dev-read-secrets" \
  ttl="1h"


Then log in with your JWT.


Got it — you want Vault to trust only the JWT that GitLab gives to the job, check the important GitLab claims (like project_path and ref), and then issue a short-lived Vault token that can read just the secrets for that project/branch.

Let’s wire that up.

I’ll show you:

What GitLab gives you

How to configure Vault’s JWT auth for GitLab

How to lock it down by project and branch (project_path, ref)

How to issue a short-lived Vault token

What to put in .gitlab-ci.yml

1. What GitLab gives you

In the job, GitLab can expose a JWT (usually via CI_JOB_JWT or CI_JOB_JWT_V2 depending on your setup). That token usually has claims like:

{
  "iss": "https://gitlab.internal.lan",
  "sub": "job_12345",
  "aud": "https://vault.internal.lan",
  "project_path": "underrock/platform/api",
  "ref": "main",
  "ref_type": "branch",
  "namespace_id": "42"
}


These four are the ones you said you care about:

sub → it’s a GitLab job

aud → must match what we tell Vault

project_path → which project/pipeline this is

ref → which branch/tag

We’re going to make Vault reject the login if any of these don’t match.

2. Enable + configure JWT in Vault
vault auth enable jwt


Now tell Vault how to verify GitLab’s tokens.

If your GitLab is internal and has an OIDC/JWKS endpoint:

vault write auth/jwt/config \
  oidc_discovery_url="https://gitlab.internal.lan" \
  default_role="gitlab-ci"


If Vault can’t reach GitLab (air-gapped), export the GitLab JWKS/public key and give it to Vault:

vault write auth/jwt/config \
  jwt_validation_pubkeys=@gitlab-jwks.pem \
  default_role="gitlab-ci"


Key point: Vault must be able to cryptographically verify the GitLab token. That’s the root of the trust.

3. Create a policy for that project

Say this project should only read:

secret/data/gitlab/underrock/platform/api/*

Create a policy:

# gitlab-underrock-platform-api.hcl
path "secret/data/gitlab/underrock/platform/api/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}


Load it:

vault policy write gitlab-underrock-platform-api gitlab-underrock-platform-api.hcl

4. Create the Vault JWT role with bound claims

Here’s the important part. We’ll tell Vault:

accept JWTs only from your GitLab (bound_issuer)

accept JWTs with this audience (bound_audiences)

accept JWTs only when project_path matches your GitLab project

accept JWTs only when ref is the branch you want (e.g. main)

issue only a short-lived Vault token

vault write auth/jwt/role/gitlab-underrock-platform-api \
  role_type="jwt" \
  bound_issuer="https://gitlab.internal.lan" \
  bound_audiences="https://vault.internal.lan" \
  user_claim="sub" \
  bound_claims='{
    "project_path": "underrock/platform/api",
    "ref": "main"
  }' \
  policies="gitlab-underrock-platform-api" \
  ttl="600s"


What this does:

If the GitLab job is from a different repo → denied

If the job runs on a different branch → denied

If someone copies the JWT but changes aud → denied

Vault token lifetime = 10 minutes (you can go 300s / 900s depending on job length)

You can also add a namespace/org check if you like:

  bound_claims_type="string"


or add:

  claim_mappings="project_path:project_path"


but for your use case, the strict bound_claims is enough.

5. Test with curl

Assume the GitLab job token is in $CI_JOB_JWT_V2. Locally you can simulate:

JWT="$(cat gitlab-job.jwt)"

curl -s \
  --request POST \
  --data "{\"role\": \"gitlab-underrock-platform-api\", \"jwt\": \"$JWT\"}" \
  http://127.0.0.1:8200/v1/auth/jwt/login


You should get:

{
  "auth": {
    "client_token": "hvs.xxxxx",
    "policies": ["default", "gitlab-underrock-platform-api"],
    "lease_duration": 600,
    "renewable": true
  }
}


That client_token is what your CI job will export and use for the rest of the steps.

6. GitLab CI example

Here’s a minimal .gitlab-ci.yml that:

grabs Vault token using the job’s JWT

reads the secret

fails if claims don’t match (Vault will 403)

stages:
  - build

build:
  stage: build
  image: alpine:3.19
  script:
    - apk add --no-cache curl jq

    # 1) login to Vault using GitLab's JWT
    - |
      VAULT_ADDR="https://vault.internal.lan"
      VAULT_ROLE="gitlab-underrock-platform-api"
      LOGIN_RESP=$(curl -s --request POST \
        --data "{\"role\": \"${VAULT_ROLE}\", \"jwt\": \"${CI_JOB_JWT_V2}\"}" \
        "${VAULT_ADDR}/v1/auth/jwt/login")

      # exit if error
      echo "$LOGIN_RESP" | jq -e '.auth.client_token' >/dev/null || {
        echo "Vault login failed:"
        echo "$LOGIN_RESP"
        exit 1
      }

      VAULT_TOKEN=$(echo "$LOGIN_RESP" | jq -r '.auth.client_token')

    # 2) read a secret in that project scope
    - |
      SECRET_RESP=$(curl -s \
        -H "X-Vault-Token: ${VAULT_TOKEN}" \
        "${VAULT_ADDR}/v1/secret/data/gitlab/underrock/platform/api/app-config")

      echo "$SECRET_RESP" | jq -e '.data.data' >/dev/null || {
        echo "Failed to read secret"
        echo "$SECRET_RESP"
        exit 1
      }

      # extract real values
      APP_KEY=$(echo "$SECRET_RESP" | jq -r '.data.data.APP_KEY')
      echo "Got APP_KEY (redacted)"

    # continue build using APP_KEY...


Why this is secure:

The job never stores a long-lived Vault token.

If someone replays the same GitLab JWT from another project or branch, Vault will reject it because of bound_claims.

Vault token lifetime is tiny (10 min).

7. Variants

Multiple branches
If you want to allow main and release/*, make the role less strict and add another role for release:

vault write auth/jwt/role/gitlab-underrock-platform-api-release \
  role_type="jwt" \
  bound_issuer="https://gitlab.internal.lan" \
  bound_audiences="https://vault.internal.lan" \
  user_claim="sub" \
  bound_claims='{
    "project_path": "underrock/platform/api",
    "ref_type": "branch"
  }' \
  policies="gitlab-underrock-platform-api" \
  ttl="600s"


Then in CI pick the role based on $CI_COMMIT_REF_NAME.

Per-project secrets
Just create one policy + one role per project and bind to project_path.

8. What you said you wanted to guarantee

“access to secrets should only be granted if the claims are valid and authorized”

You already have it with this combo:

Vault JWT config → proves the token was signed by GitLab

bound_issuer + bound_audiences → proves the token was actually meant for Vault

bound_claims on project_path + ref → proves it’s the right project/branch

Policy → limits what can be read

Short ttl → makes the resulting Vault token short-lived

That’s the standard, clean setup for “GitLab → Vault” using JWT.

